import
	../../html

intro-more-overloading html[]()
	- section "Multiple expected types",
		- p quote
			Due to overloading, an expression can have multiple expected types.
			{br}
			In most cases, this is basically like having no expected type, but in some cases it can matter.
			{br}
			Below, {num-1} has two expected types {nat8} and {string}.
			It chooses {nat8} since a number literal can't be a {string}.
			{br}
			With no expected type, the type would have been {nat} instead.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log foo 1

			foo nat8(a nat8)
				a * 10
			foo string(a string) # not used
				a

	- section "Effects of left to right type checking",
		- p quote
			Since type checking works from left to right,
			if you do need type annotations, it's best to put them on earlier arguments.
			{br}
			This gives the type checker information earlier.

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/bits

			main void()
				# 'a' is 'nat8'
				a = foo 0::nat8, 1
				log count-ones ~a
				# 'b' is 'nat64' ('1::nat8' implicitly converts to 'nat64')
				b = foo 0, 1::nat8
				log count-ones ~b

			foo[t] t(a t, _ t)
				a

		- aside-small quote
			It's even better to annotate the return type:
			{var-a}{sp}{nat8}{sp}{keyword "="}{sp}{fn-foo}{sp}{num-0}{comma}{num-1}

	- section "More examples",
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log foo::string bar

			foo nat(a int) # this is not used
				a.to
			foo string(a symbol)
				log "Using foo string(a symbol)"
				a.to
			bar int() # this is not used
				1
			bar symbol()
				log "Using bar symbol()"
				"a"

		- p quote
			Keen type-checks function calls from left to right (assuming prefix syntax). So:
		- bullets
			- quote
				If first sees a call to {fn-foo} expecting a {string} (due to the {colon-colon}{string} at the call).
				{br}
				There is only one {fn-foo} function that returns a {string}, so the other overload is discarded.
			- quote
				Then it checks the argument to {fn-foo} expecting a {symbol},
				since that is the parameter type of the only surviving {fn-foo} overload.
			- quote
				That argument is a call to {fn-bar}.
				{br}
				There is only one {fn-bar} returning a {symbol}, so the other overload is discarded.
			- quote
				After checking the call to {fn-bar},
				it now has an argument type {symbol} and finishes checking the call to {fn-foo}.
				{br}
				The argument type matches the remaining {fn-foo}'s parameter type, so it's done.

		- p quote
			Another example:
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log foo bar

			foo string(a bool) # This is not used
				"\{a\}"
			foo string(a symbol)
				log "Using foo string(a symbol)"
				a.to
			bar int() # this is not used
				1
			bar symbol()
				log "Using bar symbol()"
				"a"

		- bullets
			- quote
				If first sees a call to {fn-foo}.
				{br}
				There are two {fn-foo} functions,
				and it can't discard any based on the number of arguments or return type.
			- quote
				Then it checks the argument to {fn-foo} expecting a {bool} or {symbol},
				since those are the parameter types of the {fn-foo} functions.
			- quote
				That argument is a call to {fn-bar}.
				Only the {fn-bar} returning {symbol} matches one of the expected types.
				{br}
				The {fn-bar} returning {int} is discarded as it can't match {bool} or {symbol}.
			- quote
				After checking the call to {fn-bar},
				it now has an argument type {symbol} and finishes checking the call to {fn-foo}.
				{br}
				Since only one {fn-foo} declares a {symbol} parameter, it uses that one.
